## Lists

ordered, mutable collection of items, defined with square brackets
can hold mixed types in the same list — numbers, strings, even other lists
example: my_list = [1, 2, 3, "four", 5.0]
common operations: my_list.append(6)
my_list.insert(0, "start")
my_list.remove("four")
my_list.pop()

## Tuples

like a list but immutable — once created, it can't be changed
defined with parentheses
example: my_tuple = (1, 2, 3)
why bother with them over lists: faster, safer when data shouldn't change by accident, and can be used as dictionary keys where lists can't
a single-item tuple needs a trailing comma: (1,) — without it, (1) is just the number 1 in brackets

## Sets

unordered collection of unique items, duplicates get dropped automatically
defined with curly braces or set()
example: my_set = {1, 2, 2, 3} becomes {1, 2, 3}
handy for stripping duplicates out of a list: unique_items = set([1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3])
supports proper set operations: a = {1, 2, 3}
b = {2, 3, 4}
a | b union result {1, 2, 3, 4}
a & b intersection result {2, 3}
a - b difference result {1}

## Dictionaries

key-value pairs, defined with curly braces
example: my_dict = {"name": "Ben", "age": 20}
values accessed by key, not by position: my_dict["name"] result "Ben"
adding or updating: my_dict["age"] = 21
my_dict["city"] = "London"
keys have to be unique and immutable — strings, numbers, and tuples work fine as keys, lists don't

## Strings as sequences

a string behaves like a sequence of characters, so a lot of list-style operations work on it directly
can be indexed, sliced, looped over, checked for membership
example: word = "python"
word[0] result "p"
word[-1] result "n"
for letter in word:
print(letter)
unlike lists though, strings are immutable — a character can't be changed in place, only replaced by building a new string

## Indexing

grabbing a single item out of a sequence (list, tuple, string) by its position
positions start at 0, not 1
negative numbers count backwards from the end
example: items = ["a", "b", "c", "d"]
items[0] result "a"
items[-1] result "d"

## Slicing

grabbing a range of items instead of just one
syntax: sequence[start:stop:step] — start is included, stop is not
example: items = [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
items[1:4] result [1, 2, 3]
items[:3] result [0, 1, 2]
items[3:] result [3, 4, 5]
items[::2] result [0, 2, 4] every second item
items[::-1] result [5, 4, 3, 2, 1, 0] reversed

## Nested data structures

structures containing other structures — lists of lists, dictionaries containing lists, lists of dictionaries, and so on
basically what real-world data looks like (think JSON)
example: people = [
{"name": "Ben", "hobbies": ["gaming", "coding"]},
{"name": "Sam", "hobbies": ["reading"]}
]
people[0]["hobbies"][1] result "coding"
reaching a deep value just means chaining indexes and keys together, one layer at a time

## Mutability vs immutability

mutable = can be changed after it's created — lists, dicts, sets
immutable = locked in once created — tuples, strings, numbers
example showing the difference: my_list = [1, 2, 3]
my_list[0] = 99 fine, lists are mutable my_tuple = (1, 2, 3)
my_tuple[0] = 99 raises an error, tuples are immutable
matters a lot with function arguments — pass a mutable object into a function and the function can quietly change the original

## Copying

writing new_list = old_list doesn't actually copy anything, both names just point at the same list
example of that trap: a = [1, 2, 3]
b = a
b.append(4)
print(a) result [1, 2, 3, 4] a changed too, even though only b was touched
to make an actual copy: .copy(), list(), or slicing with [:] b = a.copy()
b = a[:]
with nested structures (a list of lists, say), a normal copy only copies the outer layer — the inner lists are still shared between both. For a full, independent copy, use deepcopy() from the copy module

## Membership

checking whether something exists inside a collection, using in and not in
example: fruits = ["apple", "banana", "cherry"]
"banana" in fruits result True
"grape" not in fruits result True
works on lists, tuples, sets, strings (checks for a substring), and dictionaries (checks keys by default)
checking membership in a set is much quicker than in a list, especially with a large collection

## Sorting

sorted() returns a brand new sorted list, leaves the original untouched
.sort() sorts a list in place and overwrites the original — only works on lists
example: nums = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5]
sorted(nums) result [1, 1, 3, 4, 5], nums itself unchanged
nums.sort() nums is now [1, 1, 3, 4, 5]
nums.sort(reverse=True) result [5, 4, 3, 1, 1]
can sort by a custom rule using key: words = ["banana", "kiwi", "apple"]
sorted(words, key=len) result ["kiwi", "apple", "banana"] sorted by length

## Searching

.index() returns the position of the first match, raises an error if there isn't one
in only tells you whether something exists, not where
example: items = ["a", "b", "c"]
items.index("b") result 1
searching a dictionary for a key is fast (it's hashed), searching for a value means looping through manually — dictionaries aren't built for that

## Unpacking

pulling items straight out of a sequence into named variables
example: a, b, c = [1, 2, 3]
a is 1, b is 2, c is 3
use * to catch "everything else" as a list: first, *rest = [1, 2, 3, 4]
first is 1, rest is [2, 3, 4]
also the trick behind swapping two variables without a temp variable: a, b = b, a

## Comprehensions

a compact way to build a collection straight from a loop, on one line instead of several
comes in a few flavours depending on what you're building

## List comprehension

squares = [x**2 for x in range(5)]
result [0, 1, 4, 9, 16]

evens = [x for x in range(10) if x % 2 == 0]
result [0, 2, 4, 6, 8]

## Dictionary comprehension

squares = {x: x**2 for x in range(5)}
result {0: 0, 1: 1, 2: 4, 3: 9, 4: 16}

## Set comprehension

unique_lengths = {len(word) for word in ["cat", "dog", "goat"]}
result {3, 4}

## Generator comprehension

gen = (x**2 for x in range(5))
next(gen)   result 0
next(gen)   result 1
looks almost identical to a list comprehension but uses round brackets instead of square ones
the difference is it doesn't build the whole collection in memory at once — it hands over one value at a time, only when asked for it, which matters when working with a dataset too big to hold in memory as a full list